Boolean Constructor Functions - true
(XQuery)
Article • 02/28/2023
Applies to:  SQL Server
Returns the xs:boolean value True. This is equivalent to xs:boolean("1") .
This topic provides XQuery examples against XML instances that are stored in various xml type
columns in the AdventureWorks database.
The following example queries an untyped xml variable. The expression in the value() method
returns Boolean true() if "aaa" is the attribute value. The value() method of the xml data type
converts the Boolean value into a bit and returns it.
In the following example, the query is specified against a typed xml column. The if  expression
checks the typed Boolean value of the <ROOT > element and returns the constructed XML,
accordingly. The example performs the following:
Creates an XML schema collection that defines the <ROOT > element of the xs:boolean
type.
Syntax
fn:true() as xs:boolean  
Examples
A. Using the true() XQuery Boolean function
DECLARE @x XML  
SET @x= ''<ROOT><elem attr="aaa">bbb</elem></ROOT>''  
select @x.value('' if ( (/ROOT/elem/@attr)[1] eq "aaa" ) then fn:true() else 
fn:false() '', ''bit'')  
go  
-- result = 1  

<!-- PAGE 31061 -->

Creates a table with a typed xml column by using the XML schema collection.
Saves an XML instance in the column and queries it.
