    '### SQL

<ManuStep ProdModelID="7" ProductModelName="HL Touring Frame">  
  <Location LocationID="20" SetupHours="0.15"   
              MachineHours="2"  LaborHours="1.75" LotSize="1">  
  <Steps>  
   <Step>Assemble all frame components following blueprint 1299.</Step>  
     ...  
  </Steps>  
 </Location>  
</ManuStep>    
B. Find all the material and tools used at the second work
center location in the manufacturing of a product
```sql
SELECT Instructions.query(''  
```

    declare namespace AWMI="https://schemas.microsoft.com/sqlserver/2004/07/adventure-
works/ProductModelManuInstructions";  
   <Location>  
      { (//AWMI:root/AWMI:Location)[1]/@* }  
       <Tools>  
         { for $s in (//AWMI:root/AWMI:Location)[1]//AWMI:step//AWMI:tool  
           return  
              <Tool>  

<!-- PAGE 31087 -->

Note the following from the previous query:
The query constructs the <Location>  element and retrieves its attribute values from the
database.
It uses two FLWOR (for...return) iterations: one to retrieve tools and one to retrieve the
material used.
This is the result:',
